  "purpose": "coverage-probe",
  "root_module": "Probe",
  "notes": "NOT A CIRCUIT. This is a synthetic design whose only job is to use every field of design-model.schema.json at least once, so that an arm which cannot express something fails the gate instead of waiting for a corpus that happens to hit it.\n\nIt exists because that is exactly what went wrong: the Starlark baseline could not express `exclude_from_bom` on a component without a `hardware_kind` — `.hardware()` was the only setter and it demands a kind — so the flag was silently dropped on the round trip, and the cross-arm agreement gate passed because neither benchmark used that combination. An expressiveness gap that only shows up when a design happens to hit it is not caught by a gate, it is caught by luck.\n\nWhat this covers, deliberately: all five quantity forms (exact, relative tolerance, absolute tolerance, nominal-with-asymmetric-interval, bare interval); all four value tags; a `part abstract` whose every constraint is T9-2-recoverable, so the arms need a spelling for a binding with nothing left in it; a resolved part that also carries constraints; an L9 flag that CONTRADICTS the library (a test point deliberately kept in the BOM) and one with no hardware kind at all; `dnp` and `board_only`; a nested module with input, output and `nc` ports; labelled nets with both domain attributes, unlabelled two- and three-member nets, and a single-member net (the L9b case); and both assertion tiers including a one-sided bound.\n\nNo anchor, because there is nothing external to anchor a synthetic design to — which is what `purpose: coverage-probe` declares. Its electrical content is meaningless and no measurement is reported over it.",
  "modules": [
    {
      "name": "Leg",
      "qualified_name": "probe.Leg",
      "ports": [
        { "name": "p_in", "role": "input" },
        { "name": "p_nc", "role": "nc" },
        { "name": "p_out", "role": "output" }
      ],
      "instances": [
